---
/**
 * /problems/ — CLUSTER HUB. keyword-plan:251 ("What your stucco is
 * telling you"). Built 2026-08-31.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS: five problem pages shipped under /problems/
 * and the directory itself had no index, so every one of them was an
 * orphan under a URL that 404'd. This is a NAVIGATION page. It exists
 * to route a reader to the right existing page and to make the five
 * into a crawlable cluster.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THIS IS NOT A SERVICE PAGE, and it is not a content page either.
 * Schema is CollectionPage — NOT Service, and NOT Article, because it
 * makes no argument of its own. It carries no FAQPage: every question
 * belongs to a child page and duplicating one here would compete with
 * the page that owns it.
 *
 * OWNER CONSTRAINTS FOR THIS BUILD, all held:
 *   - NO new problem topics. Exactly five entries, one per SHIPPED
 *     page. If a sixth problem page ships, add it here; until then
 *     this page must not name a symptom it cannot route.
 *   - NO new service claims. The hub sells nothing and describes no
 *     work. It links to money pages that carry their own claims.
 *   - NO unsupported facts. Every description below is drawn from the
 *     child page's own title/description/quick-answer. Nothing is
 *     asserted here that is not already argued and sourced there.
 *   - Existing SiteHeader/SiteFooter used unchanged.
 *   - HEADER NAV NOT MODIFIED (owner instruction). This page is
 *     currently reachable only by direct URL and by the in-body links
 *     added to the five children. Adding "/problems/" to the nav in
 *     SiteHeader.astro is the obvious follow-up when authorized.
 *
 * CANNIBALIZATION CONTROL — THE WHOLE RISK ON A HUB IS THAT IT
 * COMPETES WITH ITS OWN CHILDREN. Each entry gets ONE sentence of
 * symptom and ONE free self-check, both compressed from the child's
 * existing quick answer, then hands off. It deliberately does NOT
 * reproduce any field guide, verdict table, FAQ, or diagnostic
 * reasoning. If this page ever grows enough substance to rank for a
 * symptom term itself, it is stealing from the page it exists to
 * feed. Kept deliberately short for that reason.
 *
 * PLUS standing brand constraints: no thermal imaging as proof; NOT a
 * home inspection (FL licenses inspectors separately); no structural
 * or engineering assessment; no warranty, licence number, or phone;
 * no height/access claim; no crew size or project history.
 * NO PRICE RANGE renders, so `pricingDisclaimer` is NOT imported.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * VISUAL SYSTEM (redesigned 2026-09-02): migrated from this page's own local
 * hero/band CSS onto the shared Orlando interior-page components - PageHero,
 * SectionHead, Breadcrumbs - and the global utilities in Layout.astro
 * (.section, .slab, .joint, .grid-hairline, .card-link, .on-dark). COPY,
 * FACTS, SCHEMA, URLS, HEADING TEXT AND INTERNAL LINKS ARE UNCHANGED by that
 * migration; only the markup carrying them moved.
 *
 * The five entries now use .card-link - the whole cell is the link, which is
 * what the homepage cards do, so the hub reads as the same object. That
 * collapses the old markup's DOUBLE link per entry (an <a> inside the <h3>
 * plus a separate "Read the full guide" line) into one. Every destination and
 * every visible string is preserved; only the number of anchors wrapping them
 * changed, which is an accessibility improvement rather than an IA change.
 *
 * This page keeps its hub register - single-column hero, no aside CTA, no
 * FAQ - and must NOT grow diagnostic substance. See the cannibalization note
 * above; that constraint outranks any visual consistency argument. Do not
 * re-add a local hero or band rule here.
 */
import LeadForm from '../../components/LeadForm.astro';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import MobileStickyBar from '../../components/MobileStickyBar.astro';
import ContactCta from '../../components/ContactCta.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import { business, serviceArea } from '../../config/site';

const title =
  'What Your Stucco Is Telling You | Daytona Beach & Volusia County';
const description =
  'Cracks, stains, bubbling paint, hollow spots, storm damage — what each one means, and a free check you can run yourself before anyone quotes you for a repair.';

/**
 * One entry per SHIPPED page. `check` is the free self-test from that
 * page's own quick answer, compressed to a clause. Ordered by how
 * commonly the symptom brings someone to the site, cracks first.
 */
const problems = [
  {
    href: '/problems/cracks-in-stucco/',
    name: 'Cracks in stucco',
    seen: 'Lines in the wall — hairline, stepped, or running from the corner of a window.',
    check:
      'Cracks are graded by their company, not their width. What sits alongside one decides whether it is ordinary or worth acting on.',
  },
  {
    href: '/problems/water-stains-on-stucco/',
    name: 'Stains and chalky white marks',
    seen: 'Darkened patches, rust-coloured runs, or a powdery white bloom on the surface.',
    check:
      'Whether a mark comes back is more useful than what it looks like. Something is either sitting on the wall or being delivered to it.',
  },
  {
    href: '/problems/bubbling-peeling-paint-stucco/',
    name: 'Bubbling or peeling paint',
    seen: 'Blisters, flaking, or paint lifting away from the wall in sheets.',
    check:
      'A bubble tells you which layer failed. Whether it yields to a thumb separates a wall problem from a coating problem, and one of those is far better news.',
  },
  {
    href: '/problems/soft-spots-hollow-stucco/',
    name: 'Hollow sounds and soft spots',
    seen: 'A drummy note when you tap the wall, or an area that gives under your thumb.',
    check:
      'Extent matters more than the noise. An isolated area and a whole elevation that sounds the same way point at completely different things.',
  },
  {
    href: '/problems/hurricane-storm-stucco-damage/',
    name: 'Damage after a storm',
    seen: 'New-looking cracks, impact marks, or damp that has not dried since the weather came through.',
    check:
      'Storms reveal more than they cause. The inside of a crack dates it, and dating it changes what you should do next.',
  },
];

/**
 * CollectionPage with an ItemList of the five children. Not Article —
 * this page makes no argument. Not Service — it sells nothing.
 */
const collectionSchema: Record<string, unknown> = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'What your stucco is telling you',
  description,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: business.name },
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'ItemList',
    itemListElement: problems.map((p, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: p.name,
      url: p.href,
    })),
  },
};
if (serviceArea) collectionSchema.about = serviceArea;
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(collectionSchema)} />

  <SiteHeader current="/problems/" />

  <main id="main">
    {/*
      HERO. Dark PageHero, single column - no aside. This is a navigation hub,
      not a money page: it sells nothing, and the conversion affordance is the
      ContactCta at the foot. Eyebrow, h1 and lede are the same three strings
      the old local hero rendered. Breadcrumbs are new markup only and emit
      BreadcrumbList schema from Breadcrumbs.astro.

      The secondary action points at /stucco-repair/ - the work page, not one
      of the five children, since singling out one child would undercut the
      hub's whole job of routing evenly. It is not a self-link.
    */}
    <PageHero
      crumbs={[{ href: '/problems/', label: 'Stucco problems' }]}
      eyebrow="Stucco problems · Daytona Beach & Volusia County"
      title="What your stucco is telling you"
      lede="Five things a wall does when something is wrong, and what each one usually means. Every page below starts with a check you can run yourself, for nothing, before anyone quotes you for anything."
      actions={[
        { href: '/contact/', label: 'Request an estimate' },
        { href: '/stucco-repair/', label: 'Stucco repair & costs', variant: 'secondary' },
      ]}
    />

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <LeadForm />
  </div>
</section>

    {/* THE INDEX — the whole point of the page */}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead eyebrow="The five" title="Start with what you can see" />

        {/*
          .card-link cells - the whole cell is the anchor, matching the
          homepage cards. The visible strings are unchanged from the previous
          markup; what changed is that one <a> now wraps them instead of two
          pointing at the same href.
        */}
        <div class="grid-hairline cols-2 entries">
          {problems.map((p) => (
            <a class="card-link" href={p.href}>
              <h3>{p.name}</h3>
              <p class="seen">{p.seen}</p>
              <p>{p.check}</p>
              <span class="card-go">Read the full guide &rarr;</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* WHERE THESE LEAD — routing, not selling */}
    <section class="section slab joint">
      <div class="wrap">
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Where these lead"
          title="If you already know what you are looking at"
          lede="These pages diagnose. The ones below do the work."
        />
        <ul class="routes">
          <li>
            <a href="/stucco-repair/">Stucco repair</a> &mdash; what the work
            involves, and{' '}
            <a href="/stucco-repair/#pricing">typical ranges for the area</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/stucco-moisture-testing/">Moisture testing</a> &mdash;
            for when the question is whether water is behind the wall right
            now.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/stucco-water-damage-repair/">Water damage repair</a>{' '}
            &mdash; when something has already been getting in for a while.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note">
          If none of the five above matches what you are seeing, get in touch
          rather than guessing from a list. Photos help and go by email &mdash;
          a wall is easier to read than a description of one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section on-dark">
      <div class="wrap">
        <ContactCta heading="Not sure which of these you have?" />
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />

  <MobileStickyBar />
</Layout>

<style>
  /*
   * PAGE-SPECIFIC ONLY. The hero, bands, section heads, card cells and
   * breadcrumbs come from the shared components and the global utilities in
   * Layout.astro; those rules were deleted here rather than duplicated. What
   * remains is this page's own furniture: the "what you see" line that leads
   * each card, and the routing list.
   */

  /* The symptom line sits at full ink so it reads before the explanation. */
  .entries .seen { color: var(--ink); }

  .note { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.9375rem; max-width: 60ch; }

  .routes {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.125rem;
    max-width: 60ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .routes li + li { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
</style>
